  "description": "Learn Oxytocin: The Bonding Molecule in one minute.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A mother holds her newborn and both brains flood with oxytocin—a chemical handshake sealing the bond.",
      "visual": "👩‍👦"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Oxytocin is released during physical touch, breastfeeding, hugging, and social bonding moments.",
      "visual": "🤗"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "It increases trust, generosity, and the ability to read emotions in others' faces.",
      "visual": "😊"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Oxytocin also fuels in-group favoritism—it strengthens bonds with allies but increases suspicion of outsiders.",
      "visual": "🏘️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "It's not purely a 'love hormone'—it amplifies whatever social context you're in, positive or defensive.",
      "visual": "🔊"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Oxytocin bonds you to your tribe. Just remember: it defines who's in and who's out.",
      "visual": "🔵"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is a lesser-known effect of oxytocin?",
    "options": [
      "It can increase suspicion of outsiders",
      "It only works during sleep",
      "It reduces all social behavior"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
